WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.967 --> 00:00:02.101
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:02.101 --> 00:00:03.502
We're here with
Senator Bernie Sanders.

00:00:03.502 --> 00:00:05.605
There have been a lot of major
labor victories this year,

00:00:05.605 --> 00:00:07.506
but you have been lamenting
that we don't,

00:00:07.506 --> 00:00:10.943
in the modern era,
seem to have as many labor songs

00:00:10.943 --> 00:00:15.148
in popular music as we have
in previous eras

00:00:15.148 --> 00:00:18.017
of the pro-labor movement.
Does that bum you out?

00:00:18.017 --> 00:00:19.685
You've reached out and basically
said to artists,

00:00:19.685 --> 00:00:22.155
"Let's have some."
-Well, you know,

00:00:22.155 --> 00:00:24.123
you don't want to hear me sing.
That you don't want to hear.

00:00:24.123 --> 00:00:25.592
No.

00:00:25.592 --> 00:00:28.494
But what I know, and you know,
and I think most people know

00:00:28.494 --> 00:00:33.232
is that a great song
can capture the souls of people

00:00:33.232 --> 00:00:35.001
and bring people together.

00:00:35.001 --> 00:00:37.236
And you think about
Woody Guthrie and Pete Seeger

00:00:37.236 --> 00:00:42.208
and all of those people,
and the music that they wrote

00:00:42.208 --> 00:00:46.779
and that they sang spoke to
what was going on in the country

00:00:46.779 --> 00:00:48.547
and the need for
human solidarity,

00:00:48.547 --> 00:00:50.250
for everybody supporting
everybody else

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:53.185
and for people coming together
to stand up and fight, you know,

00:00:53.185 --> 00:00:55.121
for justice.
So I love that stuff.

00:00:55.121 --> 00:00:58.524
I'm a big fan of Woody Guthrie,
all these guys.

00:00:58.524 --> 00:01:03.896
And what we are going to do is
try to get the best songwriters

00:01:03.896 --> 00:01:07.867
and musicians that we can --
we can find to talk about

00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:10.203
contemporary issues,
not the 1930s,

00:01:10.203 --> 00:01:13.139
but what's going on right now
in 2022

00:01:13.139 --> 00:01:15.141
and see what we can do
with that.

00:01:15.141 --> 00:01:17.877
-Do you listen to any sort of
non-pro-labor music?

00:01:17.877 --> 00:01:19.812
[ Laughter ]

00:01:19.812 --> 00:01:22.248
-Occasionally, yeah.
-Occasionally.

00:01:22.248 --> 00:01:23.649
Hey, I want to ask
about your brother.

00:01:23.649 --> 00:01:26.452
So your brother -- you were over
sort of doing rallies

00:01:26.452 --> 00:01:28.988
for the rail workers
in the U.K.,

00:01:28.988 --> 00:01:31.423
and your brother is a politician
over there.

00:01:31.423 --> 00:01:32.959
This is your brother, Larry.
-Yes, it is.

00:01:32.959 --> 00:01:34.260
-Larry Sanders.
-Yep.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:37.096
-When you grew up, was it a --
is this a surprise,

00:01:37.096 --> 00:01:39.198
that you both got into
progressive politics?

00:01:39.198 --> 00:01:41.467
-Well, I would not be sitting
here with you today

00:01:41.467 --> 00:01:43.235
if it wasn't
for my brother.

00:01:43.235 --> 00:01:46.172
My brother bought --
my parents --

00:01:46.172 --> 00:01:49.074
my father dropped out of school
when he was 14.

00:01:49.074 --> 00:01:51.411
And we didn't have a whole lot
of books in the house.

00:01:51.411 --> 00:01:57.016
And my brother really shaped
my education in many respects.

00:01:57.016 --> 00:01:59.219
So I love him very much.

00:01:59.219 --> 00:02:01.520
Just had the opportunity to see
him and a lot of my nieces

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:04.123
and nephews in London,
so we had a great time.

00:02:04.123 --> 00:02:08.194
Did you know, by the way --
so this is his logo,

00:02:08.194 --> 00:02:10.563
and I'm wondering if he asked
or who had it first,

00:02:10.563 --> 00:02:11.831
because that's --

00:02:11.831 --> 00:02:13.699
[ Laughter ]

00:02:13.699 --> 00:02:16.535
He has been active in
the Green Party over there.

00:02:16.535 --> 00:02:19.338
And he was on
the Oxfordshire county council

00:02:19.338 --> 00:02:20.506
for a number of years.

00:02:20.506 --> 00:02:22.775
-Did you guys ever threaten
your parents

00:02:22.775 --> 00:02:24.710
as a form of rebellion

00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:26.112
of maybe being conservative
politicians?

00:02:26.112 --> 00:02:28.214
-Did we form a union?
A brothers union?

00:02:28.214 --> 00:02:29.248
-Go on strike? No.

00:02:29.248 --> 00:02:31.317
-Unionize.
-[ Laughs ]

00:02:31.317 --> 00:02:34.387
I remember, you guys, 'cause at
the time, your allowance was --

00:02:34.387 --> 00:02:35.587
it was unlivable.

00:02:35.587 --> 00:02:36.622
You couldn't have
a livable wage.

00:02:36.622 --> 00:02:38.457
-Go on strike
for more allowance.

00:02:38.457 --> 00:02:40.727
That's an idea. Now it's gonna
spread all over this country.

00:02:40.727 --> 00:02:44.062
I see kids now, organizing.

00:02:44.062 --> 00:02:47.199
You've had the pleasure of
campaigning for each other

00:02:47.199 --> 00:02:48.434
over the years.

00:02:48.434 --> 00:02:50.003
That must be a surreal moment,

00:02:50.003 --> 00:02:52.271
to get up and be able to talk
about somebody you probably know

00:02:52.271 --> 00:02:54.907
better than anyone to voters.

00:02:54.907 --> 00:02:57.543
-Well, you know, we grew up
in a 3 1/2 room,

00:02:57.543 --> 00:02:59.912
rent-controlled apartment
in Brooklyn.

00:02:59.912 --> 00:03:01.514
Y'all heard of Brooklyn?

00:03:01.514 --> 00:03:03.749
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:03.749 --> 00:03:06.185
-That's the first anyone here
thought you were from New York.

00:03:06.185 --> 00:03:08.053
[ Laughter ]

00:03:08.053 --> 00:03:09.122
-It's the Vermont accent.

00:03:09.122 --> 00:03:12.124
-It's so thick,
your Vermont accent.

00:03:12.124 --> 00:03:15.094
-But, you know, I used to,
you know, go tramping around

00:03:15.094 --> 00:03:17.329
with him, knocking on doors
and stuff, when we were young.

00:03:17.329 --> 00:03:19.465
-So he's been in the U.K.
for quite a while.

00:03:19.465 --> 00:03:22.835
Has he lost his Brooklyn accent?

00:03:22.835 --> 00:03:25.338
-It's kind of a weird mixture

00:03:25.338 --> 00:03:27.640
of English accent
plus Brooklyn accent.

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:28.874
-'Cause, you know, that's a --
it's a real --

00:03:28.874 --> 00:03:30.609
-But I can't understand
my nieces and nephews.

00:03:30.609 --> 00:03:31.611
I got to say, that's hard.

00:03:31.611 --> 00:03:33.279
[ Laughter ]

00:03:33.279 --> 00:03:35.214
-Well, we definitely
appreciate your honesty.

00:03:35.214 --> 00:03:37.483
-So, what -- you know,
I think -- you know,

00:03:37.483 --> 00:03:39.519
if I can just bring it back to
midterms one more time,

00:03:39.519 --> 00:03:43.689
it does seem like, you know, the
left has been,

00:03:43.689 --> 00:03:46.693
I don't know, energized by the
worst possible reason,

00:03:46.693 --> 00:03:47.827
which was Dobbs.

00:03:47.827 --> 00:03:49.429
And then you have --

00:03:49.429 --> 00:03:52.365
so maybe the left is surging
ahead based on abortion,

00:03:52.365 --> 00:03:55.234
but it does seem like the right
dominates the economy,

00:03:55.234 --> 00:03:57.370
as it gets closer
and closer to an election.

00:03:57.370 --> 00:04:00.573
What do politicians in the
Democratic Party have to do?

00:04:00.573 --> 00:04:03.309
-Look, what you have to say is,
be honest

00:04:03.309 --> 00:04:06.211
about what Republicans
stand for.

00:04:06.211 --> 00:04:08.714
Republicans have made clear,
Mitch McConnell and others,

00:04:08.714 --> 00:04:10.182
that they want to cut
Social Security,

00:04:10.182 --> 00:04:12.351
Medicare, and Medicaid.

00:04:12.351 --> 00:04:16.555
I think that's a horrible thing
to do, but it's bad politically.

00:04:16.555 --> 00:04:21.260
The entire world is worried
about the crisis

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:24.430
of climate change, and
these guys are really indebted

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:25.598
to the fossil fuel industry.

00:04:25.598 --> 00:04:27.066
They're not gonna do anything
on it.

00:04:27.066 --> 00:04:30.303
Many of us want to raise the
minimum wage to a living wage.

00:04:30.303 --> 00:04:34.173
I introduced legislation
for 15 bucks an hour,

00:04:34.173 --> 00:04:35.441
which is not enough now.

00:04:35.441 --> 00:04:39.278
We had zero Republican support.

00:04:39.278 --> 00:04:43.048
So I think if you look
at the Republican Party,

00:04:43.048 --> 00:04:46.518
issue by issue, you know,
it is clear

00:04:46.518 --> 00:04:48.821
that they are the party
that represents the wealthy

00:04:48.821 --> 00:04:50.122
and the powerful.

00:04:50.122 --> 00:04:52.825
Are the Democrats doing enough
for working people?

00:04:52.825 --> 00:04:55.828
No. But I think the contrast
between the two

00:04:55.828 --> 00:05:00.600
is strong enough that I would
hope that all over this country,

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:03.802
people reject
this right-wing extremism

00:05:03.802 --> 00:05:07.740
and put Democrats back in office
in the House and the Senate.

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:10.643
-Well, I really appreciate you
making the time, as always.

00:05:10.643 --> 00:05:12.211
Keep up the good fight in D.C.
For us,

00:05:12.211 --> 00:05:14.146
and we'll see you next time.
-Okay, thank you.

00:05:14.146 --> 00:05:15.381
-Senator Bernie Sanders,
everybody.

